      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the sale price of the shirt after the initial 20% discount.\n2. Calculate the final price of the shirt after applying the additional 25% discount.\n3. Determine the single percent discount that would achieve the same final price as the combined discounts.\n\nLet's start with the first step:\n\n1. The initial price of the shirt is $30. The sale price after a 20% off discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 - 0.20 \\times 30 = 30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n   So, the sale price is $24.\n\n2. The final price after applying a 25% discount on the sale price is:\n   \\[\n   24 - 0.25 \\times 24 = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n   So, the final price is $18.\n\n3. To find the single percent discount that would give the same final price, let's denote the single percent discount by \\( x \\). This means that after applying an \\( x \\)% discount, the final price is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) = 18\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) = 18 \\implies 1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6 \\implies \\frac{x}{100} = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4 \\implies x = 40\n   \\]\n   So, the single percent discount that would give the same final price is 40%.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{40}\n\\]",
