      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after the two successive discounts, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the first discount:**\n   The original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20%. Therefore, the discount amount is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 30 \\times 0.20 = 6\n   \\]\n   So, the sale price after the first discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the second discount:**\n   The second discount is 25% off the sale price of $24. The discount amount is:\n   \\[\n   24 \\times \\frac{25}{100} = 24 \\times 0.25 = 6\n   \\]\n   So, the final price after both discounts is:\n   \\[\n   24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price:**\n   Let \\( x \\) be the single percent discount equivalent to the two successive discounts. The final price after a single percent discount \\( x \\% \\) on the original price is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)\n   \\]\n   We know this must equal the final price of $18, so we set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) = 18\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 - 0.6 = \\frac{x}{100}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   0.4 = \\frac{x}{100}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
