      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the final price of the shirt after applying the successive discounts and then find the single percent discount that would give the same final price.\n\n1. **Calculate the price after the first discount:**\n   The regular price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20% off, so we calculate 20% of $30.\n   \\[\n   0.20 \\times 30 = 6\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the price after the first discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the second discount:**\n   The price after the first discount is $24. The second discount is 25% off, so we calculate 25% of $24.\n   \\[\n   0.25 \\times 24 = 6\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the price after the second discount is:\n   \\[\n   24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the final price:**\n   The final price of the shirt is $18.\n\n4. **Find the single percent discount that would give the same final price:**\n   Let \\( x \\) be the single percent discount. This means the final price is 82% of the original price (since \\( 100\\% - 82\\% = 18\\% \\) is the final price). So, we set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   0.82 \\times 30 = 18\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left side:\n   \\[\n   24.6 = 18\n   \\]\n   This equation is incorrect because we need to find the single discount that directly reduces the price to $18. However, we can also calculate the single percent discount by considering the combined effect of both discounts.\n\n   The effective discount can be calculated by considering the total percentage reduction. The first discount is 20%, and the second discount is 25% of the reduced price. So, the effective discount is:\n   \\[\n   20\\% + 25\\% - 40\\% = 75\\%\n   \\]\n   This means the single percent discount is 75%. Therefore, the final answer is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{75}\n   \\]",
